VARTOOLS — Windows Installation Notes
======================================

This file covers Windows-specific installation details.  The main ``README``
covers platform-independent material (dependencies, general ``configure``
options, ``pyvartools``, etc.); read it first.

VARTOOLS is a POSIX-oriented program.  Two parts of the code assume a POSIX
environment and **will not build on native Windows without significant
modification**:

* The embedded ``-python`` command (``src/runpython.c``) uses ``fork()`` and
  ``socketpair(AF_UNIX, ...)`` to talk to a child Python process.
* The embedded ``-R`` command (``src/runR.c``) uses the same mechanism.

Almost everything else (threading, dynamic loading, I/O) is portable.

**Recommended path: Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL).**  WSL provides a
full Linux environment, with the ``fork`` / ``socketpair`` POSIX APIs that
VARTOOLS needs, directly on Windows 10 and Windows 11.  All features of
VARTOOLS work on WSL without any code modifications.

An alternative path via **MSYS2 / MinGW-w64** is possible for users who
need a native Windows build, but comes with caveats — see section 3.


1. WSL path (recommended)
-------------------------

1.1 Install WSL
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

On Windows 10 (version 2004+) or Windows 11, open PowerShell or Command
Prompt as Administrator and run::

    wsl --install

This installs Ubuntu by default.  Reboot when prompted.  For a different
distro, run ``wsl --list --online`` and then
``wsl --install -d <DistroName>``.

Microsoft's full WSL documentation is at
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/.

1.2 Build VARTOOLS inside WSL
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Open your WSL distribution (e.g. "Ubuntu" from the Start menu) and follow
the instructions in ``README.linux`` as you would on native Linux.  All
optional dependencies install cleanly through ``apt-get``, and all VARTOOLS
features work.

1.3 Interop with Windows files
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Windows drives appear at ``/mnt/c/`` etc. inside WSL, so you can run
``vartools`` on light curves that live on the Windows side::

    vartools -i /mnt/c/Users/you/lightcurves/1.lc -LS 0.1 10.0 0.1 1 0

For best performance keep working files inside the WSL filesystem
(``~/`` etc.), not on ``/mnt/c/`` — the WSL-to-Windows filesystem bridge
adds significant overhead on large batches.


2. MSYS2 / MinGW-w64 path
-------------------------

`MSYS2 <https://www.msys2.org/>`_ provides a POSIX-like build environment
on Windows, including GCC, autotools, and many of the libraries VARTOOLS
depends on.  Core VARTOOLS compiles and runs under MSYS2.

**Major limitation:** the embedded ``-python`` and ``-R`` commands will
not work under MSYS2 or any native Windows build.  Their implementation
in ``src/runpython.c`` and ``src/runR.c`` assumes Unix-domain sockets and
``fork()``, which are not available on native Windows.  Using these
commands requires WSL.

2.1 Install MSYS2 and prerequisites
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Install MSYS2 from https://www.msys2.org/ and open the **MINGW64** shell
(not the generic MSYS2 shell — the toolchain differs).  Then::

    pacman -Syu       # update package database; may need to close and reopen the shell
    pacman -Syu       # second pass to complete updates
    pacman -S --needed \
        mingw-w64-x86_64-gcc \
        mingw-w64-x86_64-gcc-fortran \
        mingw-w64-x86_64-make \
        mingw-w64-x86_64-libtool \
        mingw-w64-x86_64-cfitsio \
        mingw-w64-x86_64-gsl

For ``./autogen.sh`` support::

    pacman -S --needed autoconf automake

``pthread`` support is provided automatically by the ``mingw-w64-x86_64-gcc``
package — no separate install is needed.  (The old
``pthreads-win32`` / ``pthreads4w`` project, mentioned in older VARTOOLS
documentation, is no longer required under modern MSYS2.)

2.2 CSPICE
~~~~~~~~~~

Build CSPICE from source, as on the other platforms.  Use NAIF's
``PC_Cygwin_GCC_64bit`` distribution (also works under MSYS2/MinGW-w64)::

    mkdir -p ~/src && cd ~/src
    curl -O https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/toolkit//C/PC_Cygwin_GCC_64bit/packages/cspice.tar.Z
    gunzip cspice.tar.Z
    tar -xf cspice.tar
    cd cspice
    sh makeall.csh

2.3 Build VARTOOLS
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

From the MINGW64 shell, inside the unpacked VARTOOLS source directory::

    ./configure \
        --prefix=$HOME/.local \
        --with-cspice=$HOME/src/cspice \
        --without-python \
        FC=gfortran F77=gfortran
    make
    make install

**Important:**  pass ``--without-python`` (and equivalently avoid
``--with-RHOME``) to skip the features that do not work on native Windows.
Without the flag, ``configure`` may try to detect Python / R and either
fail the build or produce a binary that crashes at runtime when you call
``-python`` or ``-R``.

If you attempted to build with these features and hit compilation errors
referencing ``fork``, ``socketpair``, ``AF_UNIX``, or ``SOCK_STREAM``,
this is why.

Verify::

    $HOME/.local/bin/vartools.exe -help


3. Native Windows (Visual Studio)
---------------------------------

Building with Microsoft's native toolchain (Visual Studio, ``cl.exe``) is
**not supported**.  VARTOOLS uses the GNU autoconf / automake build
system which requires a POSIX-like shell and assumes GCC-compatible
syntax throughout.  There are no Visual Studio project files, and
substantial porting work would be required to add them.

Use WSL (section 1) or MSYS2 (section 2) instead.


4. pyvartools on Windows
------------------------

Under WSL, follow ``README.linux`` — ``pyvartools`` works exactly as on
native Linux.

Under MSYS2 native builds, ``pyvartools`` itself (the Python wrapper
package) installs fine via ``pip``, and will drive the native ``vartools.exe``
for most commands.  The embedded ``-python`` and ``-R`` commands are
unavailable, as noted above; commands that don't rely on those features
work normally.


5. Typical problems
-------------------

* **``configure`` fails with "C compiler cannot create executables"** in
  the MSYS2 / non-MINGW64 shell — open the correct shell (the Start menu
  entry named **"MSYS2 MINGW64"**, not plain "MSYS2").

* **Compile errors referencing ``fork``, ``socketpair``, or ``AF_UNIX``**
  — you're building with ``-python`` or ``-R`` enabled on native Windows.
  Add ``--without-python`` (or disable R detection) during ``configure``,
  or switch to WSL.

* **Executable runs but immediately crashes loading a DLL** — the
  MSYS2 runtime DLLs aren't on Windows's ``PATH`` when you run the binary
  from outside the MINGW64 shell.  Either run it from the MINGW64 shell,
  or add the MSYS2 ``mingw64/bin`` directory to your Windows ``PATH``.

* **Paths with backslashes break a ``-L USERLIBS`` lookup** — VARTOOLS's
  library-name parsing looks for ``/`` as the path separator (``userlib.c``).
  MSYS2 and Cygwin translate Windows paths to forward-slash form
  automatically when their bash is used, so this usually isn't a problem,
  but if you are passing raw Windows paths (``C:\Users\...\lib.dll``)
  convert them to forward slashes first (``C:/Users/.../lib.dll``).
